From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gauthier Piarrette <gauthierpiarrette@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 16:40:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Keep an implicit pipeline cache per device on disk

vkd3d-proton with a shader cache path (every Highball launch) enforces its global_pipeline_cache mode and creates
pipelines with no VkPipelineCache at all, counting on the driver's own disk cache, which Mesa has and MoltenVK does
not. So every SPIR-V to MSL conversion ran again at every start: Microsoft Flight Simulator 2024 converts about
11,000 shaders at 100 to 140 ms each on an M4 at every boot, the critical path of its 7 to 8 minute shader
preparation (the Metal compiles behind it are served by Metal's cache from the second boot on).

Every VkDevice now keeps a pipeline cache of its own, used for pipelines created without one: read from a file at
creation, written back every 60 s when it grew and at destruction (an app a user kills loses at most a minute).
The file lives in highball/<program name> under the user's cache folder, one per device created in the process
and named with the build's pipelineCacheUUID, since readData() only accepts data from the same build on the same
GPU. HB_MVK_PIPELINE_CACHE names another folder, 0 disables. Writing goes through hbWriteAll, which holds the cache
lock across the size and data calls (a library added in between resets the size and failed every round in the
first form). A library read from cache data compiles its MSL on first use rather than in vkCreateDevice, where
MoltenVK compiled them one after the other. The pipeline cache data now also carries SPIRV-Cross patch 0004's
texture_2D_as_2D_array option, which matches() compares with the rest of the options struct: without it no entry
read from disk ever matched. And a library converted for one next stage must provide every output the new
next stage consumes: matches() only checked that the stored library's used outputs exist in the new pipeline,
so a vertex library converted for a fragment shader reading fewer inputs was reused with one reading more and
Metal refused the pipeline ("Fragment input(s) ... not written by vertex shader"): 2,000 to 6,700 refused
pipelines per boot with a warm device-wide cache, missing draws on the menu globe, against 42 without one
(MoltenVK's per-module cache never reused a library across pipelines, since vkd3d-proton creates one shader
module per pipeline). The interface key also carries the 32-bit base type: MoltenVK maps float, int and uint
outputs all to ANY32, so a fragment library converted for a vertex stage writing float4 at a location served a
pipeline whose vertex stage writes uint4 there (1,500 to 2,000 refused pipelines per flight). The file name
carries the patch's own format tag (hb3), since the pipelineCacheUUID does not change when the serialized
layout does; files of other tags are removed; and whatever a damaged file throws while it is read, the device
starts with an empty cache and the file goes (a layout change had killed the game with std::bad_alloc at
device creation). And a cached library serves one pairing of stages only: the options carry the SPIR-V hash
of the other stage (the fragment module's for the vertex and tessellation stages, the vertex module's for the
fragment stage), since with the two key fixes a flight still refused about 2,000 pipelines; whatever the key
misses, sharing across partners exposes it, and per-pairing sharing keeps the whole second-start win, a game
pairing the same shaders at every run. Measured on the M4 on 2026-10-15.

r2: the archive-size validation in MVKPipeline.mm names the padding each serialized struct is expected to
carry, and the fields this patch adds fill one padding byte in four of them (CompilerMSL::Options 7 to 6 for
texture_2D_as_2D_array, SPIRVToMSLConversionOptions 29 to 28 for hbPartnerHash, MSLShaderInterfaceVariable 3 to 2
for hbBaseType, SPIRVToMSLConversionConfiguration 109 to 108), which printed "[MVK-BUILD-ERROR] ... Missing -1
bytes" at every device creation (2026-10-16).

